<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cap-Vert</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cape Verde Peninsula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cap-Vert is the peninsula on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> that forms the westernmost point of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Named Cabo Verde by Portuguese navigators in 1444, it shelters the island of Gorée and is occupied today by the city of Dakar. Before colonial rule it was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebou people</a>, who broke away from the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> in the 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cap-vert.jpg" alt="Cap-Vert" width="320" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:STS054_STS054-94-32-north-south_orientation-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
